WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.340
Hey guys, Helen again.

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:08.639
So I know we’ve talked about Rick and Morty
a lot.

00:00:08.639 --> 00:00:12.730
But while Jared’s been lamenting the lack
of meaning in our smashole universe, I thought

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:17.320
it was time to give science a chance to shine
light on everyone’s favorite negligent grandpa

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.140
and his talented, career-savvy son-in-law.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:25.079
"I give you your new slogan."

00:00:25.079 --> 00:00:29.270
Specifically, I wanted to see what psychology
had to say about a problem we’ve all been

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:34.360
asking ourselves: Is Rick destined to be miserable
because of his intelligence?

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.430
And if so, is it better to be a Jerry?

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:42.230
If you ask 19th century author Fyodor Dostoevsky,
he would probably say no.

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:46.040
As he famously puts it in The Idiot: “It
is better to be unhappy and know the worst,

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.400
than to be happy in a fool’s paradise.”

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:53.400
Dostoevsky’s statement seems quite apt in
describing the dynamic between Jerry and Rick.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.530
Rick’s despondency is driven by his knowledge
that nothing matters, while a “fool’s

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:01.360
paradise” might as well just be Jerryboree
the Shymaliens simulation.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:09.240
"MMM human music ... I like it," Pluto or
the Titanic.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.160
So, is Dostoevsky right?

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:17.320
We’ll break it down in this Wisecrack edition
on the Psychology of Rick and Morty: Can being

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:21.490
smart make you miserable?

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:24.920
For being the smartest man in the universe,
Rick’s surprisingly relatable.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:29.190
As creator Dan Harmon puts it: “When you’re
standing in line at that bank sometimes, you

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:33.100
always feel like, none of these people understand
what a hurry I’m in, how much crap I have

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:34.880
to do today, what I’m up against.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.100
We’ve all been Rick.”

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:41.000
And as much as it sucks to admit, a lot of
us can also probably identify with his self-loathing

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.220
and self-destructive behavior.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.610
"Why do you keep doing this to us!?!?

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:45.610
I dunno Morty.

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:46.610
Maybe I hate myself.

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.850
Maybe I think I deserve to die.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:50.180
I dunno."

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:54.510
Rick is practically omnipotent, with the power
to invent, transform and destroy his way into

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.260
getting whatever he wants.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.880
Yet despite all that power, he’s miserable.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:04.250
On the other hand, there’s Jerry, who’s
a little bit dumb and a lot a bit discombobulated.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:08.229
Jerry operates best when he doesn’t have
to think too much because when he does, he

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:09.420
generally makes things worse.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:10.420
"Hi, honey, so, here's the thing-these guys-they
want to completely remove my penis and use

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:11.420
it as an alien's heart, and we just need you
to sign off on it."

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:12.420
"WHAT!?!"

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:13.420
"Uh-oh, maybe we have a problem here after
all guys.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:14.420
Yikes."

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:15.420
But even when he’s down in the deepest of
dumps, he doesn’t seem to suffer to the

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:16.420
same degree that Rick does.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:19.959
Jerry may not have street smarts or be able
to hold down a job, but he’s also not hitting

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:22.230
the bottle or questioning his place in the
universe.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:29.230
And his bar for happiness seems… low.

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:31.549
So is it better to be a Rick or a Jerry?

00:02:31.549 --> 00:02:33.379
First, let’s look at Rick.

00:02:33.379 --> 00:02:38.459
His inner turmoil leaks out in some subtle
and not-so-subtle ways, like his famous catchphrase

00:02:38.459 --> 00:02:41.359
"Morty, do you know what wubblubbdubdub means?"

00:02:41.359 --> 00:02:44.420
"That's just Rick's stupid nonsense catchphrase."

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:45.989
"It's not nonsense at all.

00:02:45.989 --> 00:02:51.980
In my people's tongue it means I am in great
pain, please help me," and, obviously, that

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.439
way-too-real suicide attempt in season 2.

00:02:54.439 --> 00:02:56.280
But is it because he’s smart?

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:00.719
The stereotype of the tortured genius is so
deeply woven into pop culture that we should

00:03:00.719 --> 00:03:03.379
wonder whether it’s overblown Hollywood
BS.

00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:06.949
The roster of famous folks from history who’ve
suffered from crushing depression and the

00:03:06.949 --> 00:03:10.780
attention given to the brilliance-madness
link by the media almost makes it seem like

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.260
depression is a prerequisite to success.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:17.059
But there are obviously plenty of brilliant
people who didn’t suffer from mental illness

00:03:17.059 --> 00:03:18.489
or were otherwise “normal.”

00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:22.959
They just aren’t as sensational to write
listicles about as, say, a guy who fell in

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:24.180
love with a pigeon.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:25.180
So is it true?

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:30.180
Does being a genius or even just purty smart
make you destined for misery?

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.310
Well, maybe.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:34.709
One study [Dr Ruth Karpinski, 2017] asked
3,700 Mensa members - or people who are so

00:03:34.709 --> 00:03:37.939
proud of their IQ scores that they joined
an organization to brag about it - if they

00:03:37.939 --> 00:03:40.120
were currently dealing with mental illness.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.549
More than a third said they were depressed.

00:03:42.549 --> 00:03:45.900
To put that in context, that’s 5x the national
average.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:50.819
If you rounded up 100 Mensa members and 100
regular people, about 6 to 7 of those regular

00:03:50.819 --> 00:03:55.269
people would probably be depressed, while
more than 30 of the Mensa members would be.

00:03:55.269 --> 00:03:59.599
A few caveats: the Mensa members were asked
to self-report on their own well-being here.

00:03:59.599 --> 00:04:03.829
Of those who reported feeling depressed, roughly
10 percent didn’t have a doctor tell them

00:04:03.829 --> 00:04:05.620
that they actually had depression.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:09.949
As anyone who spends way too much time Googling
symptoms knows, the art of self-diagnosis

00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:11.169
is far from perfect.

00:04:11.169 --> 00:04:14.650
Plus, are these Mensans representative of
all smart people?

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:15.650
Probably not.

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:19.950
Still, there might be a biological basis for
why highly intelligent people are prone to

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:20.950
depression.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:26.540
Karpinski, who ran the Mensa study, asserted
that people with IQs of 130-plus have “hyper

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:30.970
brains” - that is, they can comprehend the
world’s problems, uncertainties and possibilities

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:33.840
more easily than people with non-hyper-brains.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.690
The whole hyper brain idea was based on the
work of psychiatrist Dr. Kazimierz Dabrowski,

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:43.470
who noticed his exceptionally smart patients
had a tendency to overly contemplate everything,

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:45.160
especially the bad stuff.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:49.600
At times, they also seemed to experience deeper
depression compared to the intellectually

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:50.680
average.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:54.980
These traits were part of a group of characteristics
that he called “overexcitability.”

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:59.220
He didn’t see it as a bad thing - instead,
he thought it was predictive of the potential

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:03.100
for being able to achieve the most ideal version
of oneself.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:08.780
In 1972 he wrote: “One who manifests several
forms of overexcitability sees reality in

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:11.390
a different, stronger and more excitable manner.”

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:14.370
[Psychoneurosis is not an illness], Dabrowski
said that overexcitabilities come in five

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:19.370
flavors: excessive energy or nervousness,
deep appreciation for things like art or music,

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:25.560
being extremely curious or having an active
mind, having a vivid imagination, and finally,

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.330
extreme and complex emotional experiences.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:31.010
Rick is a textbook example of all of these
things.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:35.500
He’s still slaying aliens on the reg at
70 years old, freestyles award-winning tracks

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.500
on a whim, "You gotta get schwifty.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:43.950
You gotta get schwifty in here," ok, maybe
that’s not “appreciation,” but still

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:51.970
can be just a tiiiny bit overanalytical is
capable of thinking up and inventing entire

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:52.970
worlds.

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:53.970
"A dad makes a toilet look like R2D2 and breaks
the front page of Reddit, but I’m charles

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:54.970
manson because I gave you your own world instead
of an iPad."

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:55.970
And has seriously complicated feelings for
his family.

00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:56.970
"What is your problem?"

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:57.970
"Oh, I had all my problems removed my entitlement,
my narcissism, my crippling loneliness, my

00:05:57.970 --> 00:05:58.970
irrational attachments.

00:05:58.970 --> 00:05:59.970
They must be somewhere.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:00.970
They ain’t over here, bro."

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:01.970
While it hasn’t been tested scientifically,
some psychologists believe that people who

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:02.970
think this way are more prone to what’s
called existential depression.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:06.970
It’s exactly what it sounds like: Feelings
of hopelessness that come from wondering whether

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:08.840
life is ultimately meaningless.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:13.220
Dr. James Webb, a psychologist who studies
gifted kids, notes that while existential

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:17.840
depression can happen to anybody following
a crisis, it’s more likely to happen spontaneously

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:20.780
to people whose intelligence is above-average.

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:24.730
Rick never comes out and says he’s crushed
beneath the weight of a futile existence,

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:25.730
but he certainly comes close.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:26.730
"Am I evil?"

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:27.730
"Worse.

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:28.730
You’re smart.

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:29.730
When you know nothing matters, the universe
is yours.

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:30.730
And I’ve never met a universe that was into
it.

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:31.730
The universe is basically an animal.

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:32.730
It grazes on the ordinary.

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:33.730
It creates infinite idiots just to eat them,
not unlike your friend Timmy."

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:34.730
"Tommy."

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:35.730
"Yeah, it hardly matters now, sweetie.

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:36.730
You know, smart people get a chance to climb
on top, take reality for a ride, but it’ll

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:37.730
never stop trying to throw you.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:38.730
And, eventually, it will.

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:39.730
There’s no other way off."

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:40.730
On top of that, he’s bored senseless.

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:41.730
Virtually nothing in our own mundane world
impresses him, but his intellectual prowess

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:44.320
and imagination enable him to find temporary
reprieve from boredom by way of his portal

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:45.740
gun- aided adventures.

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:47.460
Oh, and alcohol.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.060
Contrast this with Jerry.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:53.180
Jerry possesses none of the traits psychologists
associated with hyper brain, unless you count

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:57.050
getting super stoked about rare coins, "There's
little R2-D2s where the George Washington's

00:06:57.050 --> 00:06:59.300
should be," dumb mobile games, or the Titanic.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:02.350
So does that mean he’s inherently happier?

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:04.830
But before we get there, real-life Helen here
for a second.

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:09.250
I wanna take a second to talk about this video’s
sponsor, Betterhelp- an online counseling

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:10.250
service.

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:14.750
For those of you who saw our Bo Burnham video,
you know Jared gave em a whirl and dug it.

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:19.000
Knowing I, like many people, might be able
to benefit from talking to someone on a regular

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.310
basis, he suggested I give them a try too.

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:22.350
So I did.

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.630
Stick around til the end of the video to hear
more about my experience.

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:30.110
In the meantime, go to http://Betterhelp.com/wisecrack1
to learn more...now back to Jerry.

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:34.040
Ok, so does Jerry’s Jerriness mean he’s
happier?

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:35.040
Nope.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:36.910
But - it’s complicated.

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:41.290
While some studies may suggest that being
smart makes you more likely to be depressed,

00:07:41.290 --> 00:07:44.280
others suggest that intelligence gives you
the ability to be able to unlock a lot of

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:48.750
amazing doors in life that will make you healthier,
and sometimes happier.

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:50.430
Specifically: money.

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:54.889
As pretty much all of the studies on IQ and
depression point out, being smarter is associated

00:07:54.889 --> 00:07:58.350
with greater wealth and, therefore, better
health.

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:02.550
Though sometimes it gets hard to distinguish
what causes what -- among the zillions of

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:07.150
criticisms of IQ tests is that kids who come
from well-off families are more likely to

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:09.310
be tested in the first place.

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:13.400
That being said, the relationship between
achievement, money and intelligence throws

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:19.150
a wrench in the idea that genius equals misery:
in some studies, having a higher IQ has actually

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:21.810
been associated with greater happiness.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:25.221
One study by researchers from the UK looked
at more than 6800 people and found that IQ

00:08:25.221 --> 00:08:29.970
was positively correlated with self-reported
happiness.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:34.909
Genius has long been associated with eccentricity
and few geniuses, fictional or otherwise,

00:08:34.909 --> 00:08:37.409
can live up to the eccentricity of Rick Sanchez.

00:08:37.409 --> 00:08:43.279
It’s safe to say Rick has a tendency to,
uh, overwhelm people with his intensity.

00:08:43.279 --> 00:08:46.920
Some researchers have said this is a hallmark
of people with high IQs.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:50.699
But these eccentricities may freak out the
normal-minded people around them, leaving

00:08:50.699 --> 00:08:52.890
them socially isolated.

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:57.730
Trying to roll with the normies requires minimizing
that intensity, which can lead to a so-called

00:08:57.730 --> 00:09:02.040
“crisis of self,” where conformity makes
people feel lost and confused about who they

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:03.269
really are.

00:09:03.269 --> 00:09:06.370
One could argue that Rick’s in a perpetual
crisis of self.

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:10.540
On Earth he’s forever an oddball, and while
that doesn’t seem to concern him too much.

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:11.540
"Belch."

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:14.910
It does mean he has to compromise his nature
for the sake of his relationship with his

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:17.490
family, like when he finally shows up to therapy.

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:22.161
“I have no doubt that you would be bored
senseless by therapy the same way I’m bored

00:09:22.161 --> 00:09:24.440
when I brush my teeth and wipe my tush.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:29.709
Because the thing about repairing, maintaining
and cleaning is...it’s not an adventure.”

00:09:29.709 --> 00:09:33.550
Yet just because Jerry isn’t an eccentric
genius doesn’t mean he doesn’t have his

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:35.019
own problems fitting in.

00:09:35.019 --> 00:09:37.970
He’s seriously lacking in social skills,
“Jerry, get a job, Jerry don’t network

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:42.279
at an alien wedding,” survival skills, “I'm
not proud to share this, but the truth is

00:09:42.279 --> 00:09:45.800
I just kept crawling and it kept working,”
...or skills of any kind, really.

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:49.490
He’s most successful in a career where he
doesn’t even know what he does, "I tell

00:09:49.490 --> 00:09:53.319
ya, the Galactic Federation taking over Earth
-- best thing that's ever happened to this

00:09:53.319 --> 00:09:54.319
family.

00:09:54.319 --> 00:09:57.129
I just got my sixth promotion this week, and
I still don't know what I do," and is otherwise

00:09:57.129 --> 00:10:02.009
so utterly incompetent that he has to beg
his kids for money, "Oh God, dad please don't."

00:10:02.009 --> 00:10:04.139
"I just need a few hundred dollars to get
through the month.

00:10:04.139 --> 00:10:05.790
I've got a some interviews coming up.

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:06.790
Something's gonna come through.

00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:07.790
I can feel it."

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:12.029
Ultimately, as Rick points out after the Whirly
Dirly fiasco, the only reason Jerry’s able

00:10:12.029 --> 00:10:14.899
to get by is because everyone feels sorry
for him.

00:10:14.899 --> 00:10:16.910
"You act like prey, but you’re a predator!

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:18.480
You use pity to lure in your victims!

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:19.480
That’s how you survive!

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:20.480
I survive because I know everything.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:21.480
That snakes survives because children wander
off, and you survive because people think,

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:22.480
"Oh, this poor piece of [bleep].

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:23.480
He never gets a break.

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:24.480
I can’t stand the deafening silent wails
of his wilting soul.

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:25.480
I guess I’ll hire him or marry him."

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:26.480
Rick gets his way by intimidating people,
while Jerry relies on their mercy.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:28.279
So, let’s recap: Some research suggests
that the “hyper brains” of highly intelligent

00:10:28.279 --> 00:10:32.699
people makes them more prone to depression,
mainly because they see the world in a different

00:10:32.699 --> 00:10:36.959
way and are more intense than your average
homo sapien, which is kind of a turnoff to

00:10:36.959 --> 00:10:38.189
the average Joes.

00:10:38.189 --> 00:10:41.949
But at the same time, the fact that they can
leverage their smarts for high-paying jobs

00:10:41.949 --> 00:10:45.930
that enable them to avoid the stresses of
poverty and afford better healthcare means

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:49.420
that as a group, they’re more likely to
be happier overall.

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:54.230
To our original question: Is it really better
to know the worst and be unhappy than to be

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:58.180
happy in a fool’s paradise; to be a Rick
rather than a Jerry?

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:03.029
Research says...well, that’s a frogging
subjective-smash question, and frankly, psychology

00:11:03.029 --> 00:11:04.610
doesn’t have a concrete answer.

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:06.110
THANKS PSYCHOLOGY.

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:10.790
But the limited evidence that IS out there
suggests that while you might be less likely

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:14.560
to suffer from existential depression as a
Jerry, you’re more likely to live a better

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:16.899
life overall as a Rick.

00:11:16.899 --> 00:11:18.850
But which would you rather be, Wisecrack?

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:23.310
Drop us a line in the comments below and thanks
for watching!

